Package: libnss-nostr
Section: admin
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: same
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: NSS module projecting the nostr identity authority into passwd/group
 libnss-nostr installs the read-only glibc NSS module libnss_nostr.so.2
 which projects nostr-authd's identity authority (a SQLite projection
 written atomically by the broker) into passwd/group lookups. Admins wire
 it into /etc/nsswitch.conf; the module reads its configuration from
 /etc/nss_nostr.conf.
 .
 Install nostr-login for the full broker+PAM+NSS login stack.

Package: libpam-nostr
Section: admin
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: same
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
         libpam-runtime (>= 1.0.1-6),
         nostr-authd (>= ${source:Version})
Recommends: libnss-nostr (>= ${source:Version})
Description: PAM module authenticating against the nostr auth broker
 libpam-nostr installs pam_nostr.so, the broker-backed PAM authenticator
 that consults nostr-authd via /run/nostr-auth/auth.sock. It also ships
 the pam-auth-update profile /usr/share/pam-configs/nostr so admins can
 compose pam_nostr into common-auth / common-account with
 `pam-auth-update --package` rather than editing gdm-password or
 common-auth by hand.
 .
 Semantics are the strict-deny posture (beads nostrc-o1ho): known nostr
 accounts with a failed proof are refused (default=die), unknown accounts
 fall through to Unix auth (user_unknown=ignore), and a stopped broker
 never bricks local login (authinfo_unavail=ignore).

Package: nostr-authd
Section: admin
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: Nostr authentication broker daemon
 nostr-authd is the SOCK_SEQPACKET broker that mediates authentication for
 the nostr-homed login stack. It vends CHECK_ACCOUNT / BEGIN_LOGIN /
 SUBMIT_PROOF over the privileged /run/nostr-auth/auth.sock, backs a
 local-vault provider (encrypted authority.db) and a NIP-46 external
 signer provider, and re-publishes a read-only NSS projection consumed by
 libnss_nostr.so.2.
 .
 Ships a hardened systemd unit
 (RuntimeDirectory=nostr-auth/0711, StateDirectory=nostr-auth/0700,
 ProtectSystem=strict, RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX,
 MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes) and the /etc/nostr-auth/auth.conf conffile.

Package: nostr-homectl
Section: admin
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, nostr-authd (>= ${source:Version})
Description: Headless identity-authority admin CLI for nostr-homed
 nostr-homectl is the GLib-free / FUSE-free identity-authority admin CLI
 (NOSTR_HOMED_ENABLE_CTL, decision D-11). It queries and mutates the
 authority store owned by nostr-authd -- listing accounts, exporting the
 read-only NSS projection, and driving disable/enable transitions.
 .
 Also ships nostr-homed-seed (the provisioning tool renamed from
 nh-seed-authority) in /usr/sbin, which mints the encrypted vault and
 the NSS projection for new accounts.

Package: nostr-homed-smb
Section: admin
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
         cifs-utils,
         nostr-authd (>= ${source:Version})
Recommends: samba-common-bin
Suggests: gvfs-backends
Description: SMB credential acquisition tools for nostr-homed
 nostr-smb-acquire drives BEGIN_SMB_PROOF against nostr-authd's
 /run/nostr-auth/user.sock and delivers the minted, short-lived SMB
 password as a Samba credentials= file (mode 0600) or, with --stdout,
 to a one-shot pipe. nostr-smb-mount is the shell wrapper that consumes
 that credentials file and mounts the share via mount.cifs (default) or
 gvfs (--mode gvfs).
 .
 Install this together with nostr-login for a headless server that
 authenticates SMB shares through the nostr broker.

Package: nostr-homed-domain
Section: admin
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, python3
Description: Inert winbind / Samba AD domain samples for nostr-homed
 Ships the winbind and Samba AD configuration samples used by nostr-homed
 to document a domain-joined deployment, plus the
 validate_domain_profile.py profile validator. Nothing in this package
 activates PAM or winbind -- it is inert by design and is safe to install
 on a stock host.

Package: nostrc-session-relay
Section: admin
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, glib-networking
Suggests: nostr-notify (>= ${source:Version})
Description: Per-user session-scoped local Nostr relay (Wave 4 / plan §3.2)
 nostrc-session-relay ships the socket-activated per-user relay
 (`nostr-session-relayd`) that hosts the desktop session's private
 event bus. The relay is bound to `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/nostr/relay.sock`
 (0600, SO_PEERCRED UID-equality admission) and started on demand by
 the shipped user socket + service pair.
 .
 The socket owner is the socket unit (never the service); logging out
 stops the pair and reclaims the fd (`PartOf=graphical-session.target`).
 .
 Install alongside `nostr-notify` (the natural companion — it consumes
 the socket for NIP-17 + NIP-29 background notifications). The package
 does NOT auto-enable anything; the user activates it with
 `systemctl --user enable --now nostr-session-relay.socket`.

Package: nostrc-relayd
Section: net
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: Network Nostr relay server daemon (Wave 4)
 nostrc-relayd is the network-facing Nostr relay server: a
 libwebsockets-backed relay that speaks NIP-01/11/42 over WebSockets
 and persists events into nostrdb (LMDB) when built with NostrDB
 support. Runs as a system daemon under systemd (`nostr-relayd.service`),
 anchored to `/etc/nostrc` (drop a `relay.toml` alongside the shipped
 `/usr/share/nostrc/relay.toml.example`).
 .
 The daemon is a HARDENED sibling of the per-user session relay in
 `nostrc-session-relay` (which binds a Unix socket for local
 desktop-session traffic). This package installs the *system* network
 daemon; install both if a host runs both kinds of relay.
 .
 Not auto-enabled. The operator activates it with
 `systemctl enable --now nostr-relayd.service` after seeding
 `/etc/nostrc/relay.toml`.

Package: nostr-notify
Section: admin
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
         libnostr1 (= ${binary:Version})
Recommends: nostrc-session-relay (>= ${source:Version}),
            nostr-dav (>= ${source:Version})
Description: Nostr NIP-29 + NIP-17 background notification daemon (Wave 4)
 nostr-notify ships `nostr-notify-daemon` — a background observer that
 subscribes to NIP-17 DMs (kind 1059 gift-wraps) and NIP-29 group
 events and emits `GNotification` toasts via `org.nostr.NotifyDaemon`
 on the session bus. Owns its OWN GApplication ID (never shares
 GNostr's `org.gnostr.Client`, per Finding 4 of the plan).
 .
 DM notifications are OPAQUE — the outer gift-wrap pubkey is random
 per NIP-17 so the daemon never leaks the sender; only NIP-29 group
 events show a ≤80-char preview. The daemon prefers the session-relay
 socket (`Recommends: nostrc-session-relay`) and falls back to
 `home_relays` when the socket is absent.
 .
 The shipped user service is opt-in — enable with
 `systemctl --user enable --now nostr-notify.service`.

Package: libnostr-publish0
Section: libs
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: same
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: Shared Nostr relay publish core for GNOME daemons
 libnostr-publish is the relay-side publish core shared by nostr-dav
 (and the planned nostr-share / nostr-wallet-agent): org.nostr.Signer
 proxy, libsoup-3 NIP-01 WebSocket transport, NIP-65 target resolution,
 and multi-relay OK aggregation (all-ACK outbox commit, quorum override,
 OK-wait deadline, retry backoff).
 .
 Ships libnostr-publish.so.0. In-tree consumers only; no -dev package.

Package: nostr-dav
Section: admin
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: gnostr-signer-daemon,
            nostrc-session-relay (>= ${source:Version})
Description: Localhost CalDAV / CardDAV / WebDAV bridge for Nostr (Wave 4)
 nostr-dav is the loopback (`127.0.0.1:7680`) DAV bridge that lets
 GNOME Evolution Data Server (Calendar / Contacts) and GNOME Files
 speak to Nostr events. Ships the daemon (`nostr-dav`), a user
 service unit, a support oneshot (`nostr-dav-dirs.service`) that
 creates the token + store dirs outside the daemon sandbox, and the
 D-Bus session activation file for `org.nostr.Dav`.
 .
 The WebSocket transport (plan bead tu6y) is now enabled by default;
 the daemon uses libsoup-3 for both the HTTP DAV server and the
 upstream WebSocket relay client.
 .
 Enable with `systemctl --user enable --now nostr-dav.service`. Token
 bootstrap happens through `gnostr-signer-daemon`; the token file
 lives under `$XDG_STATE_HOME/nostr-dav/`.

Package: nostr-share
Section: net
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: gnostr-signer-daemon, git
Suggests: nostr-dav (>= ${source:Version}),
          nostrc-session-relay (>= ${source:Version})
Description: Share to Nostr from any GNOME app's Open With menu
 nostr-share publishes whatever GNOME hands it as the right Nostr event:
 text and links as kind-1 notes (with r tags), Markdown and long text as
 NIP-23 articles, photos/video/audio via Blossom as kind-1 notes with
 NIP-92 imeta (or NIP-94 kind 1063), git repositories as NIP-34
 announcements, and .ics/.vcf files handed to nostr-dav. It registers
 org.nostr.Share.desktop for text, URL, media, PDF, directory, calendar
 and vCard MIME types, so it appears in every application's Open With.
 .
 Events are signed by org.nostr.Signer; image EXIF/XMP is stripped before
 upload and the exact event JSON is shown before signing. The libadwaita
 dialog opens when a display is available; `nostr-share --no-ui` and
 `--dry-run` work headless.

Package: nostr-wallet-agent
Section: net
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
         libnostr-publish0 (= ${binary:Version})
Recommends: gnome-keyring | org.freedesktop.secrets,
            desktop-file-utils
Description: Nostr Wallet Connect (NIP-47) agent for the desktop session
 nostr-wallet-agent owns the desktop's Nostr Wallet Connect pairing and
 exposes it as the session D-Bus service `org.nostr.Wallet1`
 (GetBalance, MakeInvoice, PayInvoice, LookupInvoice, ListTransactions,
 Pair, per-application budgets). Applications never see the pairing
 secret, which is kept in the Secret Service keyring; the agent identifies
 callers from their bus credentials and asks for approval in a libadwaita
 dialog whenever a payment exceeds the caller's daily budget.
 It is also the handler for lightning:, bitcoin: (BIP-21 with a
 lightning= invoice) and nostr+walletconnect: links.
 D-Bus activated through the shipped `nostr-wallet-agent.service` user
 unit; nothing is enabled at install time.

Package: nostr-nautilus
Section: gnome
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
         nostr-share (>= ${source:Version})
Recommends: nostr-seal (>= ${source:Version})
Enhances: nautilus
Description: Nostr items in the GNOME Files context menu
 nostr-nautilus is a Files (Nautilus 43+) extension that adds "Share to
 Nostr…", "Upload to Blossom…", "Encrypt for Nostr Contact…" and
 "Decrypt…" to the right-click menu, plus "Share This Repository to
 Nostr…" on the background of a git checkout. Each item launches
 nostr-share or nostr-seal, which show the preview, sign through
 org.nostr.Signer and encrypt; the extension itself holds no keys and
 makes no network calls.
 .
 It is the modern replacement for the retired seahorse-nautilus
 integration. Items only appear for file types the tools accept.

Package: nostr-settings
Section: gnome
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
         libnostr-publish0 (= ${binary:Version})
Recommends: nostrc-session-relay (= ${binary:Version}),
            nostr-notify (= ${binary:Version}),
            nostr-wallet-agent (= ${binary:Version})
Suggests: seahorse, nostr-seal (= ${binary:Version}),
          nostr-share (= ${binary:Version})
Description: Nostr Settings — preferences for Nostr on the GNOME desktop
 A libadwaita preferences window (org.nostr.Settings) that looks like a
 GNOME Settings panel and edits each Nostr desktop service's own
 configuration: Identity (signer identities, keyring, Passwords and
 Keys), Relays (session relay on/off and live statistics from
 org.nostr.SessionRelay1, storage limits, NIP-65 relay list with NIP-11
 details), Notifications (nostr-notify), Wallet (Nostr Wallet Connect
 pairing and per-app budgets through org.nostr.Wallet1), Media servers
 (BUD-03 Blossom list) and Files (nostr-seal / nostr-share defaults).
 Lists are signed by org.nostr.Signer; the app never holds a key.

Package: gnome-shell-extension-nostr
Section: gnome
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gnome-shell (>= 46~),
         gnome-shell (<< 49~)
Recommends: nostrc-session-relay (>= ${source:Version}),
            nostr-settings (>= ${source:Version})
Suggests: nostr-wallet-agent (>= ${source:Version}),
          nostr-notify (>= ${source:Version})
Enhances: gnome-shell
Description: GNOME Shell extension: Nostr relay toggle and wallet balance
 Adds a "Nostr Relay" Quick Settings toggle (next to Wi-Fi and Bluetooth)
 for the per-user session relay. Switching it uses the same systemd user
 unit operations as Nostr Settings (enable and start
 nostr-session-relay.socket; disable, then stop), and its subtitle shows
 the relay's event count and storage from org.nostr.SessionRelay1 while
 it runs. Watching the panel never starts a stopped relay.
 .
 The toggle's menu shows whether nostr-notify delivers direct-message
 notifications, the Nostr Wallet Connect balance from nostr-wallet-agent
 (only once the agent allows GNOME Shell to read the wallet; the panel
 never triggers an approval prompt by itself), and opens Nostr Settings.
 .
 Enable it with `gnome-extensions enable nostr@nostr.org` or the
 Extensions app.

Package: nostrc-samba-server
Section: admin
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
         samba,
         nostr-authd (>= ${source:Version}),
         nostr-home-fuse (>= ${source:Version})
Description: Dedicated Samba file-server for the Nostr auth broker (Wave 4)
 nostrc-samba-server is the dedicated Samba subpackage: a standalone
 `nostr-smbd.service` that runs its own `smbd` bound to
 `/etc/nostr-auth/smb.conf` against a private `tdbsam` passdb at
 `/var/lib/nostr-auth/smbpasswd`, ISOLATED from the host's default
 `samba.service`. See `SAMBA_STANDALONE.md` for the mediation
 contract.
 .
 The share path defaults to `/var/lib/nostr-auth/share-root` — the
 FUSE mount materialized by `nostr-home-fuse.service` (Wave 4 uses
 share-declaration v1, not a VFS plugin; a FUSE failure surfaces as
 share-level ENOENT / EIO, not an smbd crash).
 .
 Ships five files:
   * `/etc/nostr-auth/smb.conf` — dedicated standalone smb.conf
     (renamed from `smb.conf.standalone.sample`);
   * `/etc/nostr-auth/smb-credentiald.conf` — reserved authority
     config for the future rename-and-lift daemon (renamed from
     `.sample`);
   * `/etc/nostr-auth/servers.d/example.conf` — server-identity
     drop-in example;
   * `/lib/systemd/system/nostr-smbd.service` — dedicated smbd
     unit;
   * `/usr/lib/sysusers.d/nostr-smb-share.conf` — sysusers.d entry
     for the `nostr-smb-share` account force-user'd by the share.
 .
 The postinst DOES NOT auto-enable the unit — the operator activates
 it after seeding the passdb via `nostr-authd` and configuring their
 shares (`systemctl enable --now nostr-smbd.service`).

Package: nostr-login
Section: metapackages
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         libnss-nostr (>= ${source:Version}),
         libpam-nostr (>= ${source:Version}),
         nostr-authd (>= ${source:Version}),
         nostr-homectl (>= ${source:Version})
Recommends: nostr-homed-smb (>= ${source:Version})
Suggests: nostr-homed-domain (>= ${source:Version})
Description: Headless nostr login stack (metapackage)
 Depends on the four components that make up a working nostr login stack
 on a headless server: the NSS projection (libnss-nostr), the broker-backed
 PAM module (libpam-nostr), the auth broker daemon (nostr-authd) and the
 identity-authority admin CLI (nostr-homectl).
 .
 The dependency closure is deliberately headless (decision D-14): no
 GTK/GNOME/gvfs/libsecret/libpeas/webkit/gstreamer/FUSE, and no GLib.


# Portable-home Phase 5 packaging (bead nostrc-h10m.2 / design
# home-from-relay.md §7.2 + nostrfs-porthome-overlay.md §D9).
# The base nostr-login stack (headless) is UNCHANGED — these three
# packages are additive and only pulled in when the operator opts
# into portable homes. libhanami0 is factored out so both the sync
# and the FUSE overlay share it without either bundling the .so.

Package: libhanami0
Section: libs
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: same
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: Blossom / git / SQLite helper library (nostrc portable-home)
 libhanami is the shared substrate the nostrc portable-home stack
 links against: Blossom BUD-01/02/04 client (HTTP via libcurl),
 a Merkle-friendly SQLite index, and small git/libgit2 helpers.
 .
 Ships libhanami.so.0. Currently consumed only by nostr-home-sync
 and nostr-home-fuse; no public API commitment yet.

Package: nostr-home-sync
Section: admin
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
         libhanami0 (= ${binary:Version}),
         libnostr1 (= ${binary:Version}),
         nostr-authd (>= ${source:Version}),
         adduser
Recommends: libnotify-bin
Description: Portable-home sync daemon for nostr-homed (EXPERIMENTAL)
 nostr-home-syncd is the user-scoped sync daemon that materializes,
 tracks and re-uploads a portable $HOME snapshot to Blossom under
 kind-30078 relay pointers (design docs/designs/home-from-relay.md
 §6). It runs unprivileged as the login user under
 nostr-home-sync.service (systemd --user) and never touches system
 state.
 .
 Also ships the unprivileged fetch helper
 /usr/libexec/nostr-homed/nostr-home-fetch that nostr-authd's
 PROVISION_HOME job forks + drop-privs execs, and the sysusers.d(5)
 snippet that provisions the dedicated `nostr-home-fetch` account
 used by the sandbox (see docs/designs/home-from-relay.md §8.2).
 .
 EXPERIMENTAL: the wire format is versioned but not yet frozen; the
 daemon carries the NH_PORTHOME_EXPERIMENTAL / NH_SYNCD_EXPERIMENTAL
 compile-time flags and every public artefact is documented as such.

Package: nostr-home-fuse
Section: admin
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
         libhanami0 (= ${binary:Version}),
         libnostr1 (= ${binary:Version}),
         fuse3
Recommends: nostr-home-sync (= ${binary:Version})
Description: Read-only portable-home FUSE overlay for nostr-homed (EXPERIMENTAL)
 nostr-home-fuse mounts the current portable-home snapshot as a
 read-only view under $HOME/Portable, streaming cold subtrees from
 Blossom on demand (design docs/designs/nostrfs-porthome-overlay.md).
 The mount is torn down at logout via PartOf=graphical-session.target.
 .
 Depends on fuse3 for the setuid fusermount3 helper that performs the
 mount; the daemon itself runs as the login user and holds no
 privileges beyond the FUSE session. Requires a running
 nostr-home-syncd to produce snapshot.json; Recommends the companion
 package for that reason.
 .
 EXPERIMENTAL: gated behind NOSTR_HOMED_ENABLE_PORTHOME_FUSE_EXPERIMENTAL
 at build time; the wire format and mount policy may change.

Package: nostr-dispatcher
Section: net
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: nostrc-session-relay (>= ${source:Version})
Description: Kind-aware router for nostr: links (org.nostr.Dispatcher1)
 nostr-dispatcher is the single owner of the x-scheme-handler/nostr and
 x-scheme-handler/web+nostr URI schemes and of the Nostr event file type
 (application/vnd.nostr.event+json, *.nostr / *.nevent). It resolves
 the event kind of a NIP-21 link (NIP-19 TLV, else the session relay,
 else the link's relay hints — every fetched event is id- and
 signature-validated) and opens it in the application registered for
 that kind.
 .
 Applications declare kinds with X-Nostr-Kinds= in their .desktop file;
 users override with ~/.config/nostr/handlers.list (see
 /usr/share/doc/nostr-dispatcher/README.md). Running handlers can take
 the event directly over org.nostr.Handler1.
 .
 The org.nostr.Dispatcher1 service is D-Bus activated on demand through
 a user unit and exits when idle; nothing is enabled at install time.

Package: nostr-seal
Section: utils
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
         libnostr1 (= ${binary:Version})
Description: Encrypt files for Nostr public keys or a passphrase
 nostr-seal seals files for one or more npubs (NIP-44 v2 key agreement
 wrapping a random per-file key) or for a passphrase (NIP-49 scrypt +
 XChaCha20-Poly1305), with the body in the chunked, content-addressed
 porthome AEAD container. Files stream in bounded memory and every byte is
 authenticated.
 .
 Opening a file sealed for an npub goes through the org.nostr.Signer D-Bus
 service (NIP44DeriveConversationKey, approval-gated), so the nsec never
 leaves the signer. Ships the nostr-seal CLI, the nostr-seal-gtk
 double-click handler and the application/vnd.nostr.sealed (*.nsealed)
 MIME type.

Package: nostr-search-provider
Section: net
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: nostr-dispatcher (= ${binary:Version}),
            nostrc-session-relay (>= ${source:Version})
Enhances: gnome-shell
Description: GNOME Shell search provider for Nostr profiles and notes
 Type an npub, nprofile, note, nevent or naddr identifier, a NIP-05
 address (alice@example.com) or keywords into the Activities overview
 and get matching Nostr profiles, notes and articles from the per-user
 session relay. Activating a result opens it through nostr-dispatcher,
 so the application registered for the event kind handles it.
 Searches only the local session relay (NIP-50 search when available,
 otherwise a bounded local scan) and always answers within a deadline.
 Typed NIP-05 addresses are resolved over HTTPS and profile pictures
 are cached as small PNGs; both can be turned off in
 ~/.config/nostr/search-provider.conf.
 The org.nostr.SearchProvider service is D-Bus activated on demand
 through a user unit and exits when idle.

Package: nostr-signer-webext-host
Section: web
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Suggests: firefox | firefox-esr | chromium, nostr-wallet-agent
Description: NIP-07 browser bridge to the desktop Nostr signer (native host)
 nostr-signer-webext-host is the native-messaging host
 (org.nostr.signer_bridge) behind the "Nostr Signer Bridge" browser
 extension. The extension provides window.nostr (NIP-07) to web pages;
 every call is forwarded over native messaging to this host and from there
 to the desktop signer org.nostr.Signer on the session bus, with the page
 origin as the signer app_id, so web clients use the desktop-managed
 identity and the signer's own approval dialogs. The host holds no keys.
 With nostr-wallet-agent installed and a wallet paired, the extension also
 provides window.webln (WebLN), with per-site budgets and approvals.
 This package installs the host binary and the host manifests for Firefox
 (/usr/lib/mozilla/native-messaging-hosts), Chromium
 (/etc/chromium/native-messaging-hosts) and Google Chrome
 (/etc/opt/chrome/native-messaging-hosts). The browser extension itself is
 installed separately; see /usr/share/doc/nostr-signer-webext-host/.
